questions.question:
The following Java program attempts to create a Map<String, List<String>> that maps each city to the names of people older than 18. It throws an exception at runtime. Identify the bug and fix it so the program prints the correct mapping. Provide the corrected code.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

class Person {
    private String name;
    private String city;
    private int age;
    public Person(String name, String city, int age) { this.name = name; this.city = city; this.age = age; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public String getCity() { return city; }
    public int getAge() { return age; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Person> people = Arrays.asList(
            new Person("Alice", "London", 30),
            new Person("Bob", "Paris", 20),
            new Person("Charlie", "London", 17),
            new Person("Dave", "Paris", 25)
        );

        Map<String, List<String>> result = people.stream()
            .filter(p -> p.getAge() > 18)
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(
                p -> p.getCity(),
                p -> Arrays.asList(p.getName())
            ));

        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

questions.expected_answer:
The code fails because Collectors.toMap is used but multiple people can have the same city, causing duplicate keys and an IllegalStateException. Use groupingBy with mapping to collect names into lists per city. Corrected code:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

class Person {
    private String name;
    private String city;
    private int age;
    public Person(String name, String city, int age) { this.name = name; this.city = city; this.age = age; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public String getCity() { return city; }
    public int getAge() { return age; }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Person> people = Arrays.asList(
            new Person("Alice", "London", 30),
            new Person("Bob", "Paris", 20),
            new Person("Charlie", "London", 17),
            new Person("Dave", "Paris", 25)
        );

        Map<String, List<String>> result = people.stream()
            .filter(p -> p.getAge() > 18)
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Person::getCity,
                Collectors.mapping(Person::getName, Collectors.toList())
            ));

        System.out.println(result);
    }
}
```

Explanation: groupingBy groups elements by city; mapping extracts the name for each Person and collects them into a List. This avoids duplicate-key errors and produces a Map<String, List<String>> like {London=[Alice], Paris=[Bob, Dave]}.

questions.user_answer:
Bug:
Collectors.toMap() throws an exception because two people share the same city key (e.g., "London"), and no merge function is provided. Also, Arrays.asList() creates an immutable list. corrected code: Map<String, List<String>> result = people.stream()
    .filter(p -> p.getAge() > 18)
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Person::getCity,
        p -> new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(p.getName())),
        (list1, list2) -> { 
            list1.addAll(list2); 
            return list1; 
        }
    ));

questions.question:
Debug the following Java program. It should produce a Map<String, Employee> mapping each department to the oldest employee in that department, but it fails to compile. Identify and fix the issue so the code compiles and prints the desired map.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class Main {
    static class Employee {
        String name;
        String dept;
        int age;
        Employee(String n, String d, int a){name=n;dept=d;age=a;}
        public String getDept(){return dept;}
        public int getAge(){return age;}
        public String toString(){return name+"("+age+")";}
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Arrays.asList(
            new Employee("Alice","HR",30),
            new Employee("Bob","HR",35),
            new Employee("Carol","IT",28)
        );

        Map<String, Employee> oldestByDept = employees.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDept,
                     Collectors.reducing((e1, e2) -> e1.getAge() >= e2.getAge() ? e1 : e2)));

        System.out.println(oldestByDept);
    }
}
```

questions.expected_answer:
The problem is that Collectors.reducing(BinaryOperator<T>) produces a Collector<T,?,Optional<T>>, so the result type is Map<String, Optional<Employee>> — not Map<String, Employee>. Fix by unwrapping the Optional, for example using collectingAndThen with maxBy. Corrected code:

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class Main {
    static class Employee {
        String name;
        String dept;
        int age;
        Employee(String n, String d, int a){name=n;dept=d;age=a;}
        public String getDept(){return dept;}
        public int getAge(){return age;}
        public String toString(){return name+"("+age+")";}
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Arrays.asList(
            new Employee("Alice","HR",30),
            new Employee("Bob","HR",35),
            new Employee("Carol","IT",28)
        );

        Map<String, Employee> oldestByDept = employees.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDept,
                Collectors.collectingAndThen(
                    Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::getAge)),
                    Optional::get // safe here because each group has at least one element
                )
            ));

        System.out.println(oldestByDept);
    }
}
```

Explanation: replacing reducing(...) with collectingAndThen(maxBy(...), Optional::get) produces a Collector that returns Employee (unwrapped) for each group, matching the declared Map<String, Employee> type. Alternatively, use Optional::orElse(null) if you want to tolerate empty groups.

